WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.739
No federal bureaucrat played a

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:03.719
bigger role in 20th century law

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:05.999
enforcement than J. Edgar Hoover,

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.009
who served as the head of the FBI

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:10.049
and its predecessor agency for

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:11.429
half a century.

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:13.109
Hoover oversaw crackdowns on

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:15.299
everything from real and imagined

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:16.889
communists, and the first red scare

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:19.019
of the 1920s and its sequel

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:21.149
in the 1950s, staged

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:23.309
high profile shootouts with public

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:25.229
enemies like John Dillinger and

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:28.049
Baby Face Nelson in the 1930s,

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:29.819
surveilled Nazi and Axis

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:31.769
sympathizers during World War II,

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:33.659
infiltrated the Ku Klux Klan, and

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:35.669
pursued extralegal operations

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:37.109
against civil rights leaders and

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:40.049
anti-war protesters in the 1960s.

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:41.969
His personal vendetta against Martin

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:43.139
Luther King Jr.

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:44.699
led to one of the most shameful

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:46.619
incidents in FBI history.

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:48.449
When the bureau sent an anonymous

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:50.339
letter to King shortly before

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:51.929
he was to receive the Nobel Peace

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:54.089
Prize, encouraging him to commit

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:56.309
suicide or be exposed

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:58.589
as a serial philanderer.

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:00.239
Hoover is the subject of Yale

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:02.009
historian Beverly Gage's new

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:04.049
biography, "G-man:

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:05.999
J. Edgar Hoover and the Making of

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.859
the American Century." Gage seeks

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:09.749
to complicate and flesh out the life

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:12.119
and legacy of Hoover, who is rightly

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:14.159
notorious for often brushing

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.049
aside constitutional limits on

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:17.219
state power.

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:19.109
Like so much police tape at a crime

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:21.029
site, Yet she points out

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:22.829
that he opposed the internment of

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:24.419
Japanese-Americans during World War

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:26.399
I, undermined Senator Joe

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:27.749
McCarthy's overwrought

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:29.789
anti-communist witch hunts and

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:31.739
refused to do political surveillance

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:33.599
for Richard Nixon, inadvertently

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.459
leading to the bungled Watergate

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:37.379
break-ins and the 37th

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:39.299
president's fall from grace.

00:01:41.069 --> 00:01:42.479
"I shall resign the presidency

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:44.759
effective at noon tomorrow.".

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:46.739
To understand Hoover in all his

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:48.929
complexity, including his much

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:50.249
whispered about personal

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:52.229
relationship with his FBI colleague

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:54.479
Clyde Tolson, is to understand

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:56.429
the moral ambiguities of the country

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:58.979
he served, Gage tells Reason,

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:01.439
as well as to understand the promise

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.599
and limits of constitutional

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:05.640
government in an open society.

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:12.459
Beverly Gage.

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:13.460
Thanks for talking to Reason.

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:15.320
Thanks for having me.

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:18.399
Let's start with the elevator pitch

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:19.400
for "G-man: J. Edgar Hoover

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:22.689
and the Making of the American

00:02:22.690 --> 00:02:24.099
Century." What, what?

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:25.660
How how do you summarize this book,

00:02:25.661 --> 00:02:27.579
how do you pitch it to people

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.469
who should be interested in reading

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:30.470
it?

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:32.889
Well, it's been 30 years almost

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:34.899
since the last real crop of

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:36.189
Hoover biographies.

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:38.589
So part of the goal is just

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:40.449
to have a chance to look at all of

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:42.459
the records, all of the new history,

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:43.929
all of the new ways of thinking that

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:46.569
have come out over these decades.

00:02:46.570 --> 00:02:48.999
But I guess I had two goals in mind.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:50.829
One is to get away from what

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:51.939
I think is a little bit of our

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:54.069
caricature of Hoover as

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:56.079
this kind of one dimensional

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:58.029
villain, this figure who

00:02:58.030 --> 00:03:00.249
stood outside of American politics,

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:02.229
manipulating everyone and actually

00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:04.539
bring him back into

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:06.369
kind of a central part of the

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:08.769
story of the 20th century.

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:10.659
And then also to situate him in

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:12.399
a couple of political traditions

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:14.319
that we don't often think of as

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:16.479
going together in our own moment.

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:19.209
One is a kind of progressive,

00:03:19.210 --> 00:03:21.129
good government expert

00:03:21.130 --> 00:03:23.319
bureaucrat tradition, and the other

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:24.969
is a kind of deep social

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:25.959
conservatism.

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:27.519
And then think about the ways that

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:29.709
those two fit together in his life

00:03:29.710 --> 00:03:31.719
and then shaped a lot of the 20th

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:32.349
century.

00:03:32.350 --> 00:03:34.269
Can you dilate on

00:03:34.270 --> 00:03:35.589
that a little bit? Because this is

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:37.509
something at Reason.

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:39.369
We tend to take a fairly

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:41.019
dark view of the progressive period,

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:43.089
partly because it was

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:44.649
so socially conservative and

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:45.819
reactionary.

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:47.109
You know, when you think about

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:49.839
things like alcohol prohibition,

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:51.699
you know, on the one hand,

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:53.409
the progressives were pushing for

00:03:53.410 --> 00:03:55.389
women's rights in certain areas

00:03:55.390 --> 00:03:56.619
and in other ways they were trying

00:03:56.620 --> 00:03:59.169
to kind of control female sexuality

00:03:59.170 --> 00:04:00.909
and new and emboldened ways.

00:04:02.050 --> 00:04:03.579
Certainly with immigration, there

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:05.709
was a progressive fixation

00:04:05.710 --> 00:04:07.779
on purity and cleanliness

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:09.579
that led to a lot of anti

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:11.829
anti-immigrant sentiment

00:04:11.830 --> 00:04:13.659
and whatnot. So talk a

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:14.919
little bit about Hoover.

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:16.629
You know, as he emerges because he

00:04:16.630 --> 00:04:18.639
was born, what in what

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:20.169
year was he born again?

00:04:20.170 --> 00:04:22.629
So he was born 1895,

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:24.699
and he really came of age then

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:26.739
in the progressive era and entered

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:29.049
government in 1917

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:31.089
and sort of the peak year

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:33.279
of progressivism in many ways.

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:34.753
Which is amazing just, I'm sorry to

00:04:34.754 --> 00:04:36.759
interupt, I mean, it's

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:39.069
it is amazing when you look at the

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:41.019
you know, I mean, it would

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:42.879
be as if Babe Ruth was still

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:44.919
playing baseball in

00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:46.659
the early seventies or something,

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:48.429
you know, just that span.

00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:50.019
And I want to talk with you a little

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:51.699
bit about, you know, that term in

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:53.289
your subtitle, "The American

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:55.239
Century." But

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:56.859
so Hoover is growing up.

00:04:56.860 --> 00:04:59.289
He's he's a Washington, D.C.,

00:04:59.290 --> 00:05:00.969
hometown guy.

00:05:00.970 --> 00:05:02.799
What does it mean to be growing up

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:04.629
in the early 1900s?

